(A small interactive shell, vsh. It reads a command line, splits it into
 words, and runs the first word as a command with the rest as its
 arguments, trying it as given, then under /bin and /usr/bin. Type "exit"
 to leave.

 A word is gathered byte by byte in one buffer and copied out as a text,
 "{word}", so every word keeps its own copy and the buffer can gather the
 next one.)

Open a file at 0 for reading called 'standard input'.

Create a buffer called line.
A buffer called word is 256 bytes in size.

While true,
    print "vsh $ " without newline,
    clear line,
    read line from 'standard input' into line,
    if line is empty then, exit 0.
    (A space at the end finishes the last word, even on a line that
     arrived without a newline)
    Append " " to line,

    a list called 'command arguments' is [],
    a text called command is "",
    a text called 'finished word' is "",
    a boolean called 'we have a command' is false,
    a number called 'line length' is line's size,
    a number called position is 1,
    a number called 'this byte' is 0,
    a boolean called 'at a separator' is false,
    a boolean called 'the word is finished' is false,
    clear word,

    (A space, a tab or a newline ends a word; any other byte joins it)
    While position is less than or equal to 'line length',
        set 'this byte' to byte position of line,
        set 'at a separator' to false,
        if 'this byte' is 32 then, set 'at a separator' to true.
        If 'this byte' is 10 then, set 'at a separator' to true.
        If 'this byte' is 9 then, set 'at a separator' to true.
        If not 'at a separator' then, set byte {word's size add 1} of word to 'this byte'.
        Set 'the word is finished' to false,
        if 'at a separator' and word's size is greater than 0 then, set 'the word is finished' to true.
        If 'the word is finished' then, a text called 'finished word' is "{word}".
        (A word joins the arguments only once there is a command, so the
         command itself never lands in its own argument list)
        If 'the word is finished' and 'we have a command' then, append 'finished word' to 'command arguments'.
        If 'the word is finished' and not 'we have a command' then, set command to 'finished word', set 'we have a command' to true.
        If 'the word is finished' then, clear word.
        Increment position.
    A number called pid is 0,
    if command is "exit" then, exit 0.
    If 'we have a command' then, set pid to fork the process.
    (In the child, Execute returns only when it fails, so each failure
     falls through to the next place to look)
    If pid is 0 and 'we have a command' then,
        Execute command with arguments 'command arguments',
        a text called 'path in bin' is "/bin/{command}",
        Execute 'path in bin' with arguments 'command arguments',
        a text called 'path in usr bin' is "/usr/bin/{command}",
        Execute 'path in usr bin' with arguments 'command arguments',
        print "vsh: {command}: command not found",
        exit 127.
    (The parent waits for the child, so the next prompt comes after its output)
    If pid is greater than 0 then, set 'reaped child' to reap process pid.
    Set 'the word is finished' to false.
